WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
An API's first release is not the only security moment.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:13.000
Changes to defaults, overloads, validation, error handling, ownership, and compatibility behavior can alter assumptions at many call sites.

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.800
Versioning should be deliberate.

4
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:23.780
If an older pattern is unsafe or unclear, deprecate it with a migration path rather than leaving callers to discover safer behavior by chance.

5
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.340
Compatibility should not silently weaken security.

6
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:37.360
A new version should not accept ambiguous inputs, relax validation, broaden authority, or hide failures simply to avoid breaking callers without a reviewed policy.

7
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.840
Migration wrappers can help when they narrow behavior and make old assumptions visible.

8
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.940
They become risky when they preserve unsafe defaults forever.

9
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:56.340
Testing should include valid calls, invalid calls, denied access, boundary sizes, resource limits, and failure paths.

10
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:05.800
Parser-like APIs or structured input boundaries may benefit from defensive fuzzing at a high level to exercise unusual shapes.

11
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.920
Tooling supports review.

12
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:20.100
Compiler warnings, sanitizers, static analysis, API review checklists, documentation review, and targeted tests all help catch interface changes that could spread risk.

13
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.620
Document compatibility behavior.

14
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:32.960
Callers should know which defaults changed, which overloads are deprecated, which errors are new, and how to migrate without weakening validation or authority boundaries.

15
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.800
Feature flags should be handled carefully.

16
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:44.140
A flag that changes validation, authorization, or failure behavior should be visible, documented, and tested in both states.

17
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.980
Review new overloads as new APIs, not as harmless convenience.

18
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.760
They may accept broader types, skip validation, or make an unsafe path easier to call.

19
00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:01.520
Changelogs should name security-relevant behavior changes plainly so downstream maintainers can update assumptions.

20
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:07.560
A good workflow treats API evolution as design maintenance, not only as compatibility bookkeeping.
